import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import CodeExample from '../../components/CodeExample.astro';
const browser = `const response = await fetch("https://api.kairosarchive.net/v3/cards/C000139.json");
if (!response.ok) throw new Error(\`HTTP \${response.status}\`);
const card = await response.json();
console.log(card.name, card.rules_text);`;
const curl = `curl --fail --location \\
  --user-agent 'my-card-tool/1.0 (contact@example.com)' \\
  'https://api.kairosarchive.net/v3/cards/C000139.json'`;
---
<Docs title="Overview & rules" description="A free, read-only JSON API for Sorcery: Contested Realm. No key, no account, no server-side search.">
  <p>Kairos Archive publishes the <a href="https://github.com/sadkinglabs/sorcery-registry">Sorcery Card Registry</a> as JSON files on a CDN. Every card, every printing, every set and the history of every change has a permanent address. There is nothing to sign up for. Read this page once and you know the rules.</p>

  <h2>Where the API lives</h2>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>Base URL</dt><dd><code>https://api.kairosarchive.net</code></dd>
    <dt>Format</dt><dd>JSON, UTF-8. Card names and rules text contain characters outside ASCII (arrows, accents), so read the bytes as UTF-8.</dd>
    <dt>Methods</dt><dd><code>GET</code>, <code>HEAD</code> and <code>OPTIONS</code>. The API is read-only: any other method is refused with <code>403</code>.</dd>
    <dt>Start here</dt><dd><a href="https://api.kairosarchive.net/versions.json"><code>/versions.json</code></a> names the newest release. The bare domain redirects there too.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>Always use <code>https://</code>. The files are static, so there are no query parameters: anything after <code>?</code> is ignored.</p>

  <h2>Required headers</h2>
  <p>Scripts and apps must send a <code>User-Agent</code> that names the project and gives a way to reach you, for example <code>my-card-tool/1.0 (contact@example.com)</code>. Requests from automated clients with no <code>User-Agent</code> at all are refused with <code>403</code>. Do not let an HTTP library leave it empty.</p>
  <p>Browsers send their own <code>User-Agent</code>, so page JavaScript needs nothing extra. No <code>Accept</code> header is needed: every file is JSON.</p>

  <h2>Rate limits</h2>
  <p>JSON files are limited to <strong>30 requests per 10 seconds per IP address</strong>. Over that, the edge answers <code>429</code> for 10 seconds, then lets you through again. Images are not limited.</p>
  <p>The limit is a tripwire against fetching cards one by one, not a quota. A client that <a href="/docs/data">downloads the whole database</a> once, or reads an index file, never meets it. A client that looks up one card at a time never meets it either. What trips it is resolving a whole deck by fetching each card's file from one server at once: fetch <code>index/cards.json</code> instead, one request.</p>

  <h2>Caching</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A release root such as <code>/v3.3.0/</code> is <strong>immutable</strong>: the bytes at a path never change. It is served with a one-year <code>immutable</code> cache header. Cache it forever.</li>
    <li><code>/versions.json</code> is cached for 60 seconds. Checking it once an hour is plenty; the data changes a few times a year.</li>
    <li>Every file carries an <code>ETag</code>. Send <code>If-None-Match</code> and you get <code>304 Not Modified</code> instead of the file again.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The one rule that matters</h2>
  <p>For anything beyond a single lookup, <strong>download <code>registry.json</code> once and query your copy</strong>. It is one file with everything in it, about 6 MB stored and a fraction of that over the wire. Poll <code>/versions.json</code> to learn when a new release exists. Please do not crawl the per-object files. <a href="/docs/data">Download the data</a> walks through it.</p>

  <h2>Look up one card</h2>
  <p>Both examples follow the <code>/v3/</code> alias, which redirects to the newest v3 release. <code>fetch</code> follows the redirect on its own; <code>curl</code> needs <code>--location</code>.</p>
  <CodeExample code={browser} title="Browser · fetch one card" />
  <CodeExample code={curl} lang="bash" title="curl · the same card" />
  <p><a href="https://api.kairosarchive.net/v3/cards/C000139.json">Open Askelon Phoenix as JSON →</a></p>

  <h2>Mirrors and availability</h2>
  <p>The domain is a CDN in front of object storage, with no maintenance windows and no SLA. Every release is also a tagged GitHub release: <code>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sadkinglabs/sorcery-registry/{'{'}tag{'}'}/export/registry.json</code>, the same bytes. Because identifiers never change, a copy you already hold is never wrong, only older.</p>

